processData()'s loop is `while (true)` upstream -- real ROS nodes are killed,
not joined, so there is no exit condition at all. Give the offline bridge a
deterministic, state-based one: the worker stops after the first pass that
both started with input already closed and moved nothing.

The closure flag is read *once at the top of the pass*, never at the bottom.
Reading it at the bottom would race: the producer can push a sweep and close
input in the window between this pass scanning the buffers (finding them
empty) and this pass testing the flag, silently dropping that sweep.

Also pops the raw queues under their own mutex instead of testing emptiness
outside it, notifies the bridge on every real dequeue so push_lidar's bounded
backpressure wait is released by progress rather than by polling, and waits
for ikd-tree rebuild quiescence at each measurement-batch boundary (see
03-deterministic-ikdtree-rebuild.patch) so correspondence searches do not
depend on scheduler timing. If the fixed 100 ms initial-map window is already
complete but contains fewer than the 100 required downsampled points, fail
immediately with the relevant density settings. Additional input cannot change
that closed window, so silently buffering the rest of the dataset is both
misleading and wasteful.

